  "worked_solution": "Let $G$ be the event that the chosen student obtained the top grade, and let $A$ be the event that the student is from Group $A.$ Then ${A}^{\u2032}$ is the event that the student is from Group $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected student obtained the top grade, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(G)=P(G|A)P(A)+P(G|{A}^{\u2032})P({A}^{\u2032})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $35%$ of her students are in Group $A$ and $65%$ are in Group $B,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.35,\\,P({A}^{\u2032})=0.65.\n$$\n\n- Since $20%$ of the students in Group $A$ obtained the top grade, we have\n\n$$\nP(G|A)=0.20.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected student from either group obtained the top  grade is $0.265,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(G)=0.265.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(G|{A}^{\u2032}),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(G) & =P(G|A)P(A)+P(G|{A}^{\u2032})P({A}^{\u2032}) \\\\ 0.265 & =(0.20)(0.35)+P(G|{A}^{\u2032})(0.65) \\\\ 0.265 & =0.07+0.65P(G|{A}^{\u2032}) \\\\ 0.65P(G|{A}^{\u2032}) & =0.195 \\\\ P(G|{A}^{\u2032}) & =0.30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected student from Group $B$ obtained the top grade is $0.30.$",
